  "owner": "skeletable",
  "slug": "data-breach-laws",
  "topic": "Law",
  "title": "US data breach notification laws",
  "question": "After a data breach, how fast must a business tell people, and who else must it tell?",
  "scope": "The general breach notification law for businesses in each US state, read from official state pages where we can. This first batch covers 10 states; all 50 and DC belong here. Laws for banks, insurers, health care and government often differ and are left out. A blank means the page we could read doesn't say, never that the answer is no. New York's values come from the 2024 bill and Texas's deadline to residents from its 2019 bill, because their current code pages can't be read. California's two day counts come from a legislative committee's account of its 2025 bill, because its code site refuses our reader. Delays for law enforcement apply in most states. This records what the laws say and isn't legal advice.",
  "columns": [
    {
      "id": "statute",
      "label": "Law",
      "type": "text",
      "rule": "The section that holds the state's general breach notification duty for businesses, as the source names it."
    },
    {
      "id": "notify_days",
      "label": "Days to tell residents",
      "type": "number",
      "unit": "days",
      "rule": "The outer limit in days for telling affected residents, as stated. Blank where no number is stated. States start the clock differently, at discovery or at determining a breach happened, and the quote shows which."
    },
    {
      "id": "notify_standard",
      "label": "How fast, in the law's words",
      "type": "text",
      "rule": "The law's own words for how quickly residents must be told, apart from any day count."
    },
    {
      "id": "ag_notice",
      "label": "Attorney general told",
      "type": "boolean",
      "rule": "Yes if the source says the state attorney general or its equivalent must be told of a breach in at least some cases. Florida's Department of Legal Affairs and Oregon's Attorney General count."
    },
    {
      "id": "ag_threshold",
      "label": "Residents before the AG is told",
      "type": "number",
      "unit": "residents",
      "rule": "The number of affected residents at which the attorney general must be told, as stated. Blank where no number is stated. The quote shows whether it means that many or more than that many."
    },
    {
      "id": "ag_days",
      "label": "Days to tell the AG",
      "type": "number",
      "unit": "days",
      "rule": "The deadline in days for telling the attorney general, as stated. Blank where no number is stated. Clocks differ: some run from the breach, and others from telling residents, as the quote shows."
    },
    {
      "id": "encrypted_exempt",
      "label": "Encrypted data excepted",
      "type": "boolean",
      "rule": "Yes if the text limits the duty to unencrypted data, or leaves encrypted data out. Stolen keys can undo this in several states."
    },
    {
      "id": "harm_exception",
      "label": "Low-harm exception",
      "type": "boolean",
      "rule": "Yes if the text lets a business skip notice after finding that harm or misuse is unlikely, or only counts breaches that create a real risk."
    }
  ],
